### THE ENVELOPE OPENING SEQUENCE

When the guest taps the envelope or its opening button, execute a carefully choreographed sequence:

1. The button responds with a subtle tactile-style visual response.
2. The wax seal gently presses, catches the light and releases.
3. The envelope flap rotates open using realistic perspective and a natural transform origin.
4. The inside of the envelope becomes visible, with believable layered depth.
5. The invitation card begins to emerge from the envelope.
6. The card moves upward and slightly forward with realistic shadows and restrained easing.
7. The card settles naturally into the center of the composition.
8. The invitation unfolds or expands into its readable presentation.
9. A delicate golden light passes over the embossed details.
10. The couple's names appear in a carefully timed typography reveal.
11. The scene transitions smoothly into the complete invitation website.

Use actual HTML elements, CSS shapes, layered components, transforms, gradients, masks and shadows where appropriate.

Create believable depth using separate layers for the envelope body, flap, interior, invitation card and seal.

The animation must have natural acceleration and deceleration, elegant timing and convincing spatial relationships. The card must appear to originate inside the envelope rather than simply fade onto the screen.

Avoid childish bouncing, abrupt cuts, excessive rotation, random particles, generic fade-only animations and effects that look like a PowerPoint presentation.

Provide a discreet skip-intro control so guests can reach the invitation immediately.

The interaction must work reliably with touchscreens, mouse, keyboard and reduced-motion settings.

After the intro has been completed, do not force guests to repeat the animation whenever they navigate the page.

